{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OPERATOR QUESTIONS, round 3 — design answers (analysis only; NOT approved work).\n\n**Q: how do we make the `ours` driver's failure loud instead of silently ignored?**\n\nFirst, a correction to the framing: the driver is not merely 'set by init and hopefully still there'. `init.py:5-6` records that the ensure registry NORMALIZES merge-driver configuration, and an existing tracker 'self-heals on any ensure sweep' (`init.py:132`). So a healthy store re-converges it. The real gap is that NOTHING VERIFIES THE ATTRIBUTE AND THE DRIVER AGREE — `.gitattributes` can name a driver that git config does not define, and git then silently ignores the attribute.\n\nThree levels, strongest first:\n\n1. **Test the EFFECT, not the configuration.** In a scratch store, construct a real merge conflict on a `.bridge_state/*` path and assert our copy won. If the driver is undefined the merge conflicts and the test fails. A config assertion is a CLAIM that a setting exists; an actual merge is PROOF the behaviour works. This session produced the same lesson twice — 599e (an entire test surface passing by not running) and 24f7 (a tree-wide grep run once by hand instead of committed as a test).\n2. **Derive the check from `.gitattributes`, do not hardcode 'ours'.** Parse every `merge=<name>` pattern and assert each `<name>` is defined in git config. That catches a FUTURE `merge=foo` added without its driver, which a hardcoded ours-check would miss.\n3. **Make the ensure sweep report when it had to fix it** (APPLIED vs already-ok). Self-healing that is silent hides the fact that something upstream keeps stripping the config.\n\n**Q: how can compaction conflicts self-heal, given compaction is deterministic?**\n\nThe determinism holds for CONTENT but not for IDENTITY, and that distinction is where the fix lives. `compiled_state` is derived from the same events, and the code already drops non-deterministic keys (`updated_at`) before persisting (`compact.py:138`) — so content-determinism is already deliberately pursued. But each SNAPSHOT is an EVENT carrying its own `uuid`, so two clients folding an IDENTICAL event set emit DIFFERENT filenames. They do not conflict; they duplicate.\n\nFour moves, in order of leverage:\n\n1. **Content-address the SNAPSHOT.** Name it by a hash of the folded event set + `compiled_state` rather than a fresh uuid. Two clients folding the same set then write a BYTE-IDENTICAL file at the SAME path, which git merges cleanly as 'both added identical content'. Different fold sets hash differently and both land harmlessly. This is the smallest change that cashes in the determinism, and it turns the deterministic case from a conflict into a non-event.\n2. **Treat compaction output as a DERIVED CACHE** — the identical policy already applied to `.bridge_state/*`. The event log is the source of truth; the snapshot is an optimization. On conflict: keep the union of live events, discard BOTH snapshots, let the next compaction rebuild. Losing a compaction costs disk, never correctness.\n3. **Bias resolution toward KEEPING events.** Uncompacted state is always semantically valid, so the safe direction is resurrect, never retire. That makes the rule provably lossless — worst case is redundant work on the next pass.\n4. Longer term, centralise compaction to a single writer, which removes the cross-clone race rather than resolving it.\n\n**HAZARD that any of these must respect:** resolution has to be ATOMIC OVER THE PAIR (the SNAPSHOT and its `.retired` renames), not per-file. A per-file `merge=ours` would happily take OUR snapshot alongside THEIR retirements, dropping events our snapshot never folded. So this needs a custom merge driver or a post-merge repair that reasons about the pair — a `.gitattributes` line cannot express it.\n\nAlso worth noting for whoever implements: `compact.py:65` already contemplates 'a remote SNAPSHOT written by another agent is visible and local compaction can ...', and `compact.py:150` handles the mid-retirement crash window (SNAPSHOT written, retirement incomplete). Both are adjacent to this problem and should be read before designing the merge behaviour."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786120582147131001,"uuid":"40380cae-1438-4092-a8f3-a8fe38e84278"}